<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veracruz (city)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> is the principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in the state of the same name. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in 1519 as the Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz and later moved to its present site, it was the gateway of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> through which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> fleets sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, guarded by the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a>. It was blockaded and attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1838, captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1847 and occupied again by American forces in 1914.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veracruz-city.jpg" alt="Veracruz (city)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alejandro borbolla · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_Francisco_Javier_Clavijero.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
